<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  M365-Visio : install-M365-Visio.xml

  Visio desktop app (Visio Plan 2 subscription). Add-on to an existing
  Microsoft 365 Apps installation. Company Portal available install.

  Worked example estate:
    The device already has Microsoft 365 Apps, installed by M365-Base or
    M365-Base-Business, on Monthly Enterprise Channel, 64-bit, en-us base
    language, possibly with da-dk added.

  READ THIS BEFORE YOU CHANGE ANYTHING IN THIS FILE

  There is no Channel attribute here. There is no OfficeClientEdition attribute
  here. Both omissions are deliberate and both are load bearing.

  Per the ODT reference on Microsoft Learn: if Microsoft 365 Apps is already
  installed and the Channel attribute is not specified, the ODT matches the
  channel of the existing installation. If Microsoft 365 Apps is installed and
  OfficeClientEdition is not specified, the ODT matches the architecture of the
  existing installation.

  The previous version of this package hard-coded Channel="MonthlyEnterprise".
  On a device running Current Channel that does not fail. It re-channels the
  ENTIRE Office installation on that device, quietly, and the change lands at
  the next update cycle rather than at install time, so nobody connects the
  Visio request to the Office estate drifting. One user asking for Visio
  should not move that device's Office to a different servicing channel. See
  MIGRATION.md, defect 2.

  A DOCUMENTED CONTRADICTION YOU SHOULD KNOW ABOUT

  Two Microsoft Learn pages disagree about Version="MatchInstalled".

  The ODT configuration reference says, of the Version attribute:
      "When you use Version="MatchInstalled", the Channel attribute is
       required."

  The Microsoft 365 Apps Rangers best practices page, "Build dynamic, lean, and
  universal packages for Microsoft 365 Apps", gives this exact scenario as its
  worked example and shows Version="MatchInstalled" with NO Channel attribute,
  explaining that removing Channel is what makes the package work across all
  update channels.

  This file follows the best practices page, because that page addresses this
  precise scenario, second installs onto an existing Office, and because
  supplying Channel is the thing that causes the harm being fixed. Validate the
  behaviour in your own estate on a device that is NOT on Monthly Enterprise
  Channel before broad release. That is the test that settles it for you.

  Language matching. Language ID="MatchInstalled" with TargetProduct="All"
  installs Visio in the same set of languages already present across all
  installed products, so a user who has da-dk on their Office gets da-dk on
  their Visio without anyone requesting it twice. TargetProduct="All" is used
  rather than a specific product ID so this one file serves both the enterprise
  and the business base packages.
-->
<Configuration ID="M365-Visio">

  <Add Version="MatchInstalled" AllowCdnFallback="TRUE">
    <Product ID="VisioProRetail">
      <Language ID="MatchInstalled" TargetProduct="All" />
    </Product>
  </Add>

  <Display Level="None" AcceptEULA="TRUE" />

  <Property Name="FORCEAPPSHUTDOWN" Value="FALSE" />

</Configuration>
